    {
      "id": "0jg4zCmrf7BWB1l_KA8cW",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -1264.2326922803943,
      "y": -4804.633096052718,
      "width": 100.98264326589927,
      "height": 154.95091764225882,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b2j",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1774836397,
      "version": 160,
      "versionNonce": 1309055437,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1778038732225,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": " CLI (Command Line Interface)\n\n  You run it in a terminal like this:\n\n  python search.py --budget 2000000 --district 성수동 --min-size 100 --rooms 5\n\n  It then scrapes Naver, and prints results in the terminal — or opens browser tabs —\n  or saves a report to a file.\n\n  Pros\n  - Fastest to build\n  - No frontend work at all\n  - Easy to automate or script\n\n  Cons\n  - Awkward for non-technical teammates\n  - Hard to display maps, images, or rich property cards\n  - Not great for browsing/comparing results visually\n  - You have to remember the exact flags each time\n\n  Honest verdict for your case: CLI is a poor fit here. Your product is fundamentally\n  visual — you're browsing property listings with photos, maps, and price comparisons.\n  A terminal is the wrong canvas for that.\n\n  ---\n  Web UI\n\n  You open a browser, fill in a form (budget, location, constraints), hit search, and\n  see a results page with property cards, a map, and a scoring summary.\n\n  Pros\n  - Natural fit for property browsing\n  - Can show maps, images, ratings side by side\n  - Easy to share a results link with a teammate\n  - Feels like an actual product\n\n  Cons\n  - More to build (frontend + backend + maybe a server)\n  - Slightly slower to start\n\n  ---\n  My recommendation\n\n  Web UI, but keep the backend logic as a standalone module.\n\n  This means:\n  - The scraping/search logic lives in plain Python (or Node.js) functions\n  - A lightweight web framework (like Next.js or FastAPI + simple HTML) just wraps it\n  - If you ever want CLI later, you call the same functions from the terminal\n\n  This gives you the best of both — visual results now, and the core logic stays clean\n  and reusable.",
      "fontSize": 2.383860271419367,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": " CLI (Command Line Interface)\n\n  You run it in a terminal like this:\n\n  python search.py --budget 2000000 --district 성수동 --min-size 100 --rooms 5\n\n  It then scrapes Naver, and prints results in the terminal — or opens browser tabs —\n  or saves a report to a file.\n\n  Pros\n  - Fastest to build\n  - No frontend work at all\n  - Easy to automate or script\n\n  Cons\n  - Awkward for non-technical teammates\n  - Hard to display maps, images, or rich property cards\n  - Not great for browsing/comparing results visually\n  - You have to remember the exact flags each time\n\n  Honest verdict for your case: CLI is a poor fit here. Your product is fundamentally\n  visual — you're browsing property listings with photos, maps, and price comparisons.\n  A terminal is the wrong canvas for that.\n\n  ---\n  Web UI\n\n  You open a browser, fill in a form (budget, location, constraints), hit search, and\n  see a results page with property cards, a map, and a scoring summary.\n\n  Pros\n  - Natural fit for property browsing\n  - Can show maps, images, ratings side by side\n  - Easy to share a results link with a teammate\n  - Feels like an actual product\n\n  Cons\n  - More to build (frontend + backend + maybe a server)\n  - Slightly slower to start\n\n  ---\n  My recommendation\n\n  Web UI, but keep the backend logic as a standalone module.\n\n  This means:\n  - The scraping/search logic lives in plain Python (or Node.js) functions\n  - A lightweight web framework (like Next.js or FastAPI + simple HTML) just wraps it\n  - If you ever want CLI later, you call the same functions from the terminal\n\n  This gives you the best of both — visual results now, and the core logic stays clean\n  and reusable.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
