      "text": " Real-Life Analogy\n\n  Imagine you're searching for a hackerhouse manually right now:\n\n  1. You open Chrome\n  2. You go to land.naver.com\n  3. You type \"성수동\" in the search box\n  4. You scroll through listings\n  5. You copy interesting ones into a spreadsheet\n\n  Semi-manual means: your code does steps 1, 4, and 5 — you do steps 2 and 3.\n\n  The human handles the tricky parts (login, CAPTCHA, search form). The code handles\n  the boring parts (reading every listing, extracting data, building the results page).\n\n  ---\n  Concrete Example — What You Actually Do\n\n  Step 1 — You run the program in terminal:\n  python search.py\n\n  Step 2 — A real Chrome window pops up on your screen:\n\n  >>> Browser opened. Go to Naver Land and search for what you want.\n  >>> When results are loaded, press Enter here to extract data...\n\n  Step 3 — You manually:\n  - Go to land.naver.com\n  - Type your search (district, price range)\n  - Wait for listings to load\n\n  Step 4 — You press Enter in the terminal\n\n  Step 5 — Your code automatically:\n  - Reads all listing cards on the page\n  - Extracts price, size, location, link\n  - Builds a clean results page for you\n\n  ---\n  What It Looks Like in Code\n\n  from playwright.sync_api import sync_playwright\n\n  with sync_playwright() as p:\n      # opens a REAL visible Chrome window\n      browser = p.chromium.launch(headless=False)\n      page = browser.new_page()\n\n      # opens Naver Land — you can see this happen\n      page.goto(\"https://land.naver.com\")\n\n      # program waits — YOU search manually in the browser\n      input(\"Search for your location in the browser, then press Enter...\")\n\n      # NOW the code takes over — extracts all listing data automatically\n      listings = page.query_selector_all(\".item_inner\")\n      for listing in listings:\n          print(listing.inner_text())\n\n  ---\n  The Key Difference Visualized\n\n  HEADLESS (fully automated)          SEMI-MANUAL\n  ──────────────────────────          ──────────────────────\n  [Your code] ──────────────>         [Your code] ──> pause\n       │                                               │\n       ▼                                               ▼\n  [Invisible browser]              [Visible browser — you see it]\n       │                                               │\n       ▼                                               ▼\n  [Naver — looks like a bot]       YOU type + click manually\n                                                       │\n                                                       ▼\n                                   [Your code resumes — extracts data]\n                                   [Naver — looks like a real user]",
      "fontSize": 3.401495331611443,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": " Real-Life Analogy\n\n  Imagine you're searching for a hackerhouse manually right now:\n\n  1. You open Chrome\n  2. You go to land.naver.com\n  3. You type \"성수동\" in the search box\n  4. You scroll through listings\n  5. You copy interesting ones into a spreadsheet\n\n  Semi-manual means: your code does steps 1, 4, and 5 — you do steps 2 and 3.\n\n  The human handles the tricky parts (login, CAPTCHA, search form). The code handles\n  the boring parts (reading every listing, extracting data, building the results page).\n\n  ---\n  Concrete Example — What You Actually Do\n\n  Step 1 — You run the program in terminal:\n  python search.py\n\n  Step 2 — A real Chrome window pops up on your screen:\n\n  >>> Browser opened. Go to Naver Land and search for what you want.\n  >>> When results are loaded, press Enter here to extract data...\n\n  Step 3 — You manually:\n  - Go to land.naver.com\n  - Type your search (district, price range)\n  - Wait for listings to load\n\n  Step 4 — You press Enter in the terminal\n\n  Step 5 — Your code automatically:\n  - Reads all listing cards on the page\n  - Extracts price, size, location, link\n  - Builds a clean results page for you\n\n  ---\n  What It Looks Like in Code\n\n  from playwright.sync_api import sync_playwright\n\n  with sync_playwright() as p:\n      # opens a REAL visible Chrome window\n      browser = p.chromium.launch(headless=False)\n      page = browser.new_page()\n\n      # opens Naver Land — you can see this happen\n      page.goto(\"https://land.naver.com\")\n\n      # program waits — YOU search manually in the browser\n      input(\"Search for your location in the browser, then press Enter...\")\n\n      # NOW the code takes over — extracts all listing data automatically\n      listings = page.query_selector_all(\".item_inner\")\n      for listing in listings:\n          print(listing.inner_text())\n\n  ---\n  The Key Difference Visualized\n\n  HEADLESS (fully automated)          SEMI-MANUAL\n  ──────────────────────────          ──────────────────────\n  [Your code] ──────────────>         [Your code] ──> pause\n       │                                               │\n       ▼                                               ▼\n  [Invisible browser]              [Visible browser — you see it]\n       │                                               │\n       ▼                                               ▼\n  [Naver — looks like a bot]       YOU type + click manually\n                                                       │\n                                                       ▼\n                                   [Your code resumes — extracts data]\n                                   [Naver — looks like a real user]",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
